{"review_id":"00001788303359016607-0004098830-000001-cycle-review-1","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:15220b0eb0aba7f106f0c6c53d7f9b11b49d28a841de56c7f39722ff0babc9d6","findings":[{"finding_id":"f1-message-start-usage-dropped","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"message_start usage is never surfaced to the observer, so an interrupted stream loses all observed input/cache tokens","claim":"In `AnthropicAdapter::submit`, the SSE event match handles `ContentBlockStart`, `ContentBlockDelta`, `ContentBlockStop` and `MessageDelta`, but `SseEvent::MessageStart` falls into the catch-all `_ => {}` arm and emits no `StreamEvent::UsageDelta`. Anthropic reports `input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens` and `cache_creation_input_tokens` on `message_start`; `output_tokens` arrives later on `message_delta`. Separately, when the stream ends without a stop reason, `AnthropicHttpClient::stream_messages` returns `Err(AdapterError::Ambiguous { .. })` and discards the accumulated `StreamOutcome.usage` entirely — the error variant carries only a reason string. The two together mean that on a partial or interrupted stream the input and cache-read token counts the provider already reported are visible nowhere: not in the returned outcome (an Err), and not in any observer event. The loop then records `UsageCategories::default()` with `ambiguous: true`, so the acceptance requirement that a partial or interrupted stream 'preserves observed usage' is not met for the input and cache categories.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":600,"end":640}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/anthropic_api.rs","range":{"start":700,"end":730}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:05726a39c1f275bfeca5ad950e6d89a7bb4c3f6f048c638b82f65438154920be","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":36795,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-05726a39c1f275bfeca5ad950e6d89a7bb4c3f6f048c638b82f65438154920be-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Emit a `StreamEvent::UsageDelta(to_usage_categories(&message.usage))` on `SseEvent::MessageStart` so input and cache-read/cache-write counts reach the observer as soon as the provider reports them, and carry the partially accumulated `WireUsage` out of `stream_messages` on the ambiguous path (e.g. an `AdapterError::Ambiguous` variant or a returned `StreamOutcome` alongside the ambiguity flag) so the attempt can be recorded as ambiguous *with* the usage that was actually observed rather than as entirely unknown. Add a fixture asserting a stream cut off after `message_start` still yields the observed input/cache-read categories.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f2-resolved-model-and-refusal-category-never-persisted","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Provider-resolved model identity and refusal category live only in an in-memory map no production path reads","claim":"`AttemptMetadata` (resolved model, refusal category) is stored in `AnthropicAdapter::attempt_metadata`, an adapter-instance-local `Mutex<HashMap<..>>`. Nothing in the daemon consumes it: `persist_run_outcome` was extended only with `provider_request_id`, and the daemon integration test passes the *requested* identifier (`Some(\"claude-test-model\")`) as `model_identity`. The resolved identity is therefore never written to `usage_observations` or any other durable record, so the requirement that model identity record both the requested identifier and the provider-resolved identity is satisfied only by an in-memory API that no caller in this change invokes; the only assertions are in tests that read the map directly. The same applies to the refusal category: the loop maps `Refusal { .. }` onto `StopReason::ProviderFailure { NonRetryable }`, which carries no category, and the category is persisted nowhere — a refusal is indistinguishable in the ledger from an auth failure. Additionally, the metadata insert is guarded by `if let Some(resolved_model) = &stream_outcome.resolved_model`, so a refusal on a response whose `message_start` omitted `model` records no refusal category at all.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":628,"end":652}},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","hunk":"@@ -526,7 +527,7 @@ pub fn persist_run_outcome(\n             cli_version: None,\n             model_identity,\n             service_tier: None,\n-            provider_request_id: None,\n+            provider_request_id: provider_request_id.as_deref(),\n             uncached_input_tokens: usage.uncached_input_tokens,\n             cache_read_tokens: usage.cache_read_tokens,\n             cache_write_tokens: usage.cache_write_tokens,"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:d51193ebb30800f0b6816452275d8c79dea13c116dc2457876ac1ce14e254edf","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":97145,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-d51193ebb30800f0b6816452275d8c79dea13c116dc2457876ac1ce14e254edf-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Thread the resolved model identity and refusal category out of the adapter into the per-attempt record the same way `provider_request_id` was threaded (an additional `AttemptUsage`/`SubmitOutcome` field, or a documented host call to `attempt_metadata` inside the daemon's persistence path), and assert in a daemon-level fixture that a request for a moving alias persists the dated snapshot alongside the requested id, and that a refusal persists its category. Record the refusal category unconditionally rather than only when `resolved_model` is present.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f3-synthesized-tool-use-fabricates-empty-input","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Missing registry entry synthesizes a tool_use block with fabricated empty input","claim":"`push_assistant_and_tool_turn` falls back, when `tool_use_registry` has no entry for a `tool_result`'s `call_id`, to pushing `WireContentBlock::ToolUse { id, name: payload.capability_name, input: json!({}) }`. This fabricates assistant history the model never produced: on any fresh adapter instance resuming persisted conversation history (explicitly named as a case in the code comment), every prior tool call is replayed to the model as having been invoked with no arguments at all. The model then sees, for example, a `read-file` call with no `path` followed by a successful file-content `tool_result`, which is incoherent history and can steer subsequent turns. The registry being adapter-instance-scoped rather than conversation-scoped makes this the expected path after any process restart, not an edge case, and no fixture covers a fresh adapter instance replaying persisted history.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":362,"end":400}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:05726a39c1f275bfeca5ad950e6d89a7bb4c3f6f048c638b82f65438154920be","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":36795,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-05726a39c1f275bfeca5ad950e6d89a7bb4c3f6f048c638b82f65438154920be-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Persist the tool_use arguments alongside the tool result (or carry them on `ToolResultPayload`) so a resumed adapter instance replays the real input rather than `{}`; failing that, make the synthesized-block path explicit and observable — record that history was reconstructed without the original arguments — and add a fixture that submits persisted history through a freshly constructed adapter and asserts what is sent on the wire.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f4-retry-after-discarded-by-loop","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Parsed retry-after is discarded by the loop, so no caller can respect it","claim":"`classify_http_status` parses the `retry-after` header into `RetryableKind::RateLimited { retry_after_ms }`, but `run_loop` collapses every retryable adapter error into `StopReason::ProviderFailure { taxonomy: Retryable }`, which carries no retry-after value and no per-attempt record. The integration test asserts exactly this shape and `outcome.attempts.is_empty()`, confirming the delay is dropped at the loop boundary. The contract document states the caller 'is expected to respect it within its own budget', but the only caller in this change discards it, so the acceptance requirement that retryable errors respect retry-after within budgets is not realized end-to-end and no fixture exercises a caller acting on it.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":355,"end":395}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/anthropic_api.rs","range":{"start":555,"end":585}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:d51193ebb30800f0b6816452275d8c79dea13c116dc2457876ac1ce14e254edf","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":97145,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-d51193ebb30800f0b6816452275d8c79dea13c116dc2457876ac1ce14e254edf-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Carry the retry-after hint out of the loop on the retryable stop (e.g. `ProviderFailure { taxonomy, retry_after_ms }` or an accompanying field on `RunOutcome`) so the orchestration layer that decides whether to re-invoke can honour it, and add a fixture that asserts a 429 with `retry-after` reaches the caller with the delay intact. If threading it is deliberately deferred, state that in the contract instead of asserting the caller is expected to respect it.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f5-unbounded-adapter-local-maps","category":"maintainability_issue","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"tool_use_registry and attempt_metadata grow without bound for the life of the adapter","claim":"`AnthropicAdapter` holds two `Mutex<HashMap<..>>` caches that are only ever inserted into. `tool_use_registry` gains an entry for every completed `tool_use` block and is never removed — not on replay, not on turn completion, not by `cancel`. `attempt_metadata` gains an entry per successful attempt and is removed only by an explicit `cancel(&attempt_id)` call, which the loop's timeout path does not make (cancellation happens by dropping the future). A long-lived daemon adapter instance driving many executions therefore accumulates every tool call's parsed input JSON and every thinking block's text and signature for the process lifetime.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":128,"end":146}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":655,"end":670}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"lint","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:5ff8f35fe6f8a4d31bbef1a552a5e9931c67bff85b06d2129726489eb3653664","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":1312,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-5ff8f35fe6f8a4d31bbef1a552a5e9931c67bff85b06d2129726489eb3653664-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Bound both maps — evict a remembered tool_use once it has been replayed and its turn is behind the current history, cap the registry with an LRU, or scope both to a per-run session object dropped when the run ends. Document the intended lifetime either way, since the current comment describes the registry as instance-scoped without saying anything about when entries die.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f6-env-mutation-in-parallel-tests","category":"test_gap","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"Tests mutate process environment with std::env::remove_var while the harness runs threads in parallel","claim":"Three added tests call `std::env::remove_var` at test time — `missing_credential_fails_closed_with_exact_remedy`, `auth_failure_stops_closed_without_ever_sending_a_request`, and `env_resolver_fails_closed_with_remedy_for_missing_var`. Rust's test harness runs tests in the same process on parallel threads, and `remove_var` is not thread-safe against concurrent environment reads (it is `unsafe` from Rust 2024 onward for exactly this reason). Any concurrently running test that reads the environment — including `EnvCredentialResolver::resolve` in another test — races with these calls, making the auth fixtures a latent source of nondeterministic failures rather than the deterministic fixtures the contract document claims.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":1290,"end":1320}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/anthropic_adapter.rs","range":{"start":430,"end":460}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:05726a39c1f275bfeca5ad950e6d89a7bb4c3f6f048c638b82f65438154920be","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":36795,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-05726a39c1f275bfeca5ad950e6d89a7bb4c3f6f048c638b82f65438154920be-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Drop the `remove_var` calls and rely on a variable name that is never set (the resolver already fails closed on an absent variable), or inject the lookup through the existing `CredentialResolver` seam so the fixture never touches process-global state.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
